---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import { DISCORD_INVITE_URL } from "../consts";

const sections = [
  { href: "#overview", label: "Overview" },
  { href: "#casus-belli", label: "Casus Belli" },
  { href: "#criteria", label: "Criteria" },
  { href: "#declaration", label: "Declaration of War" },
  { href: "#engagement", label: "Rules of Engagement" },
  { href: "#war", label: "War" },
  { href: "#consequences", label: "Consequences" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Warfare · Meridian"
  description="The governing document for formal warfare between nations on Meridian: casus belli, declarations, rules of engagement, and the consequences of war."
  path="/war"
>
<main class="doc">
  <h1>Warfare</h1>
  <p class="lead">The governing document for formal warfare between nations. Anything not declared and approved here is just ordinary PvP and is covered by the <a href="/rules#pvp">Rulebook</a>.</p>

  <Callout>Every war starts with a staff-reviewed ticket in <a href={DISCORD_INVITE_URL} rel="noopener">our Discord</a>. Do not take hostile action in-game until a declaration has been approved and filed.</Callout>

  <Toc sections={sections} />

  <h2 id="overview"><span class="num">1</span>Overview</h2>
  <p>Wars are one of the most notable mechanics unique to the server, letting you raid, capture, and rob enemy territory. But you cannot simply declare war on whoever, whenever &mdash; there is a formal process for it, and this guide covers that process from start to finish.</p>

  <h2 id="casus-belli"><span class="num">2</span>Casus Belli</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Declaring war requires a <strong>casus belli</strong> (case for war). Players cannot declare war at will; it requires justification.</li>
    <li>Justification only applies when your nation or land has been provoked by another nation or land. This includes, but is not limited to:
      <ul>
        <li>Attacks on your citizens or allies</li>
        <li>Attacks on your borders</li>
        <li>Violating treaties and agreements</li>
        <li>Subjugation</li>
        <li>Failure to pay debts</li>
        <li>Excessive toxicity towards your citizens or allies</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="criteria"><span class="num">3</span>Criteria</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>You must have probable evidence to support your case.</li>
    <li>Claims must be within <strong>2,500 blocks</strong> of each other to declare war.</li>
    <li>The declarer must post a wager of gold. The amount is decided by staff.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="declaration"><span class="num">4</span>Declaration of War</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>File a war ticket in our Discord server to start the process. Staff may deny your request if they deem your case for war to be insufficient or invalid.</li>
    <li>If the casus belli is approved, the declarer decides the consequences of war &mdash; either <strong>Raid</strong> or <strong>Vassalage</strong> &mdash; and both parties agree on a time and date for the war to begin.</li>
    <li>From there, staff handle the war in-game and decide the wager amount in gold.</li>
  </ol>

  <Callout variant="warn">Refusing to engage after a war has been formally declared (&ldquo;war dodging&rdquo;) is a punishable offense.</Callout>

  <h2 id="engagement"><span class="num">5</span>Rules of Engagement</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The use of potions, arrow effects, and Mace PvP in war is banned. The only exception is Fire Resistance potions.</li>
    <li>To engage in a war, a player must have at least <strong>2 hours of playtime</strong> and a first sign-in on the server at least <strong>one week</strong> before the declaration.</li>
    <li>Excessive toxicity in wartime can result in removal from that conflict, independent of any other moderation action.</li>
    <li>Nations are responsible for their members' behavior and are expected to uphold community standards. If a nation is found to be generally toxic, staff may decide the outcome of the conflict in the opposing party's favor.</li>
    <li>Belligerents must constantly contest the field of battle for the duration of the war. Scoring a point and then intentionally abandoning the attack or defense is not permitted.</li>
    <li>Mass griefing is not permitted. This includes lava casting, obsidian walling, and flood machines.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="war"><span class="num">6</span>War</h2>
  <p>At the scheduled time and date, the war is triggered in-game and a window of <strong>48 hours</strong> begins.</p>
  <p>The first side to reach a target score wins the war. The target score is decided by player count: <strong>8 points per player, 30 points minimum</strong>. If neither side reaches the target score within 48 hours, victory goes to whoever has the most points at that point.</p>
  <p>Points are earned two ways: <strong>1 point</strong> per kill, or <strong>5 points</strong> per captured chunk of territory.</p>
  <p>Territory is captured via capture blocks &mdash; a craftable beacon placed in the defender's land. If the attackers control the area immediately around it for a set time, they capture that chunk, the adjacent chunks, and score points. Defenders can repel the attackers and reverse their capture progress; if they hold long enough, the capture block is destroyed and the defenders score points instead.</p>

  <h2 id="consequences"><span class="num">7</span>Consequences</h2>
  <p>There are two possible consequences of war, and they apply to both parties regardless of who declared it:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Raid.</strong> The winner gets access to the loser's lands for 1 hour.</li>
    <li><strong>Vassalage.</strong> The loser is subjugated and forced to pay elevated taxes to the winner.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The wager follows the outcome, not the consequence: if the declarer loses, their wager goes to the defender. If the declarer wins, they keep it.</p>
</main>
</Base>
